            <ArticleTitle>Small cell carcinoma of the liver and biliary tract without jaundice.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>8146-50</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.3748/wjg.v19.i44.8146</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>An 80-year-old woman presenting with chest pain was found to have a large, lobulated soft tissue mass in the liver and nearby tissues on abdominal computed tomography (CT). The tumor had invaded the common hepatic artery and main portal vein. Jaundice developed 4 wk later, at which point, a pancreas and biliary CT scan revealed a large mass in the right lobe of the liver and a hilar duct obstruction, which was found to be a small cell carcinoma. Despite its rarity, liver and bile duct small cell carcinoma should be considered in the differential diagnosis of atypical chest pain without jaundice.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
